Source patch for the GNU build layer only -- the Forth engine (os/unix/
wrapper.c, fsys.c) and the host tools (tools/didepend.c, comp.c, makeprom.c).

Every hunk is a declaration Sun's compiler accepted silently and GCC 14
rejects by default (-Werror=implicit-function-declaration, -Werror=implicit-int,
-Werror=return-mismatch are on since GCC 14; GCC 13 warns). Without this the
gnu path stops building on the next Ubuntu/Debian release. Twelve sites:

  implicit function declarations -- getnum, xref_generate (wrapper.c),
    xref_add_string (fsys.c). Each now has a prototype copied from its
    definition. xref_add_string is a Sun bug on its own: libxref's header
    declares xref_xref_add_string, a function that does not exist, and never
    declares the one fsys.c calls. All three return int or void, so the
    implicit `int` guessed nothing wrong on LP64 -- no codegen change.

  sigset (wrapper.c) -- the SYS5 branch #defines signal to sigset. glibc has
    sigset(3) but declares it only under _XOPEN_SOURCE>=500, and deprecated,
    so the call was implicit. That one WAS wrong on LP64: s_signal() -- in the
    function table the dictionary calls through (wrapper.c:189) -- returns
    the previous handler as a long, and an implicit int truncated the pointer
    to 32 bits. Nothing in the dictionary reads that value back, which is
    why it never showed. The #define is now skipped on Linux, where signal(2)
    already has the semantics sigset(3) was chosen for on SVR4 (handler
    stays installed, signal blocked while it runs).

  implicit int -- `static lmode` (wrapper.c, fsys.c), `static oldcode`,
    `static offset` (comp.c), executable() (wrapper.c), main() (didepend.c).
    Spelled out; no change in meaning.

  return with no value in a function returning int -- makeprom.c do_dropin(),
    on the nested-OBME early exit. Reachable only with -r (recurselevel > 0),
    which this build never passes; the caller used whatever was in the return
    register. Now returns what the function's contract says: true if the
    buffer was rewritten and should be copied back, including by the nested
    call.

Applied after engine-getmem-mprotect-exec.patch and makeprom-ptr-roundup.patch
by build/obp/gnu/engine/Makefile to a staged copy; src/obp is never touched.
Paths follow the other two: os/unix/ from src/obp/obp, tools/ from src/obp.

